A Route parameter describes a known choice constructor and the payload it will
receive later. Routes are parameter contracts, not callable values. The
following Wire/Route forms teach accepted design and are unsupported by the
current compiler until Phase 6.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    TodoMessage ::
        Rename | id Int, text String |,
        Delete | id Int |,
    ;

    offer_rename |on_rename Route of String -> TodoMessage| -> String:
        return "Rename route received"
    ;

    offer_delete |on_delete Route of TodoMessage| -> String:
        return "Delete route received"
    ;

    todo_id = 42
    offer_rename(TodoMessage::Rename(todo_id))
    offer_delete(TodoMessage::Delete(todo_id))
]

The first route has one future input, `String`; `todo_id` is captured now as the
leading payload field. The second route has no future input and captures the
complete `Delete` payload now. The receiving parameter is the immediate context
that permits a constructor to leave fields for later input. An ordinary choice
construction outside that context must provide every field.

Rows can use the same zero-input shape to retain their own item identity:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    make_row_delete_offer |id Int| -> String:
        return offer_delete(TodoMessage::Delete(id))
    ;
]

Each call forms a route with that row's `id` already captured. A row loop can
pass its current `id` to `make_row_delete_offer`; the example does not invoke
the route, register an event or provide a UI callback. Those runtime
operations are outside the accepted foundation.

Choice values still describe application state changes in the ordinary way.
This self-contained example uses currently executable Moth syntax; the update
function receives an ordinary choice value and does not use Wire or Route:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    TodoMessage ::
        Rename | id Int, text String |,
        Delete | id Int |,
    ;

    update |state String, message TodoMessage| -> String:
        return if message is:
            Rename(id, text) => then text
            Delete(id) => then state
        ;
    ;
]

A child message can be lifted into one parent choice constructor by capturing a
leading field:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    EditorMessage ::
        Changed | value String |,
    ;

    AppMessage ::
        Editor | editor_id Int, message EditorMessage |,
    ;

    receive_editor_route |on_message Route of EditorMessage -> AppMessage| -> String:
        return "Route received"
    ;

    forward_editor |on_message Route of EditorMessage -> AppMessage| -> String:
        return receive_editor_route(on_message)
    ;

    forward_editor(AppMessage::Editor(7))
]

This uses one Route. It does not compose routes or call one from another. A
compatible Route parameter can be forwarded unchanged; route invocation and
composition remain unsupported. These examples teach accepted future contracts
and are unsupported by the current compiler until Phase 6.
